## Private planning procedure

Complete these steps privately before producing the response:

1. Build a heading index from the frozen bundle, including each normalized title's per-file
   occurrence count. Copy paths and complete displayed heading titles exactly, preserving
   numbering, case, punctuation, and wording. When a title repeats, use its nearest containing
   ancestor whose title occurs exactly once; never infer or modernize titles.
2. Build an atomic semantic-requirement ledger covering behavior, constraints, failures,
   quantitative targets, future capabilities, deferrals, non-goals, and open research questions.
   Headings organize sources; they are not themselves coverage obligations.
3. Preserve every distinct requirement's modality, horizon, conditions, prerequisites, conflicts,
   and readiness effect. Collapse exact semantic duplicates into one obligation while retaining
   their occurrences privately for conflict detection.
4. Give every distinct requirement exactly one phase owner. Use a source-backed global invariant
   only as a reusable cross-cutting reference whose establishing work still has one phase owner.
5. Partition requirements with the deterministic boundary precedence below. Keep implementation,
   contracts, proofs, and checks with the behavior they establish.
6. Add only direct producer-consumer dependencies. Determine each phase's readiness from its own
   source contract; dependency completion and another phase's readiness never propagate through
   the graph.
7. Perform a second coverage pass over every source heading only to find missed semantic
   requirements; headings are not disposition units. Reverse-check every distinct material
   requirement for one visible disposition, then check every roadmap claim for source or
   verification-policy authority.

Do not emit the private ledger or heading index.

## Canonical decomposition

Derive the justified capability phases. Never assume a particular stage count, maturity model,
numbering scheme, domain, or boundary from filenames. Preserve only source-defined boundaries.

Use these rules so repeated runs over the same corpus converge:

- Resolve boundaries in this order: (1) an explicit source-defined capability or deliverable with
  its own acceptance boundary; (2) a genuine local readiness difference whose isolation leaves the
  remainder complete and non-vacuous; (3) a direct producer-consumer artifact boundary; then
  (4) merge work sharing authoritative state. A later rule never overrides an earlier one, and
  headings, APIs, modules, or independently executable work alone do not force a split.
- Keep implementation, contracts, proofs, fixtures, and checks with the behavior they establish.
  Separate cross-system evidence or assurance only when the source defines an accepted artifact,
  report, or gate consumed across multiple capabilities. Collapse generic checker,
  preregistration, and reference-deployment work into behavior owners. Every phase owns at least
  one source-backed outcome; policy alone cannot create a phase.
- Group open studies by their source-defined outcome and parallelism. They do not become production
  dependencies unless the source explicitly requires production to wait for their result.
- Never create one phase per heading, type, error, fixture, proof, or check.
- IDs are short lower-kebab-case nouns taken from the capability's first defining source occurrence.
  Use the shortest unique capability name consistently; no ordinal or `stage`, `phase`, `formal`,
  or `verified` prefix. Add the shortest containing source capability noun only to resolve a
  collision.
- List only direct dependencies. Shared vocabulary, file order, policy obligations, and a desire
  for a linear narrative are not dependencies. Independent capabilities remain independent.
- Keep graph and IDs stable. Oxide canonicalizes phase topological order, global invariants by ID,
  direct dependencies by ID, applicable global invariants by ID, and source records by canonical
  source key. For authored narrative lists (`included_scope`, `excluded_scope`,
  `implementation_goals`, and `verification_goals`), use one deterministic source or logical
  order; Oxide preserves that order.
- Use one-sentence outcomes and non-overlapping scope and goal lists. Each item states one distinct
  claim in deterministic source or logical order. Do not impose an arbitrary phase-count or
  list-length budget: include every distinct source-authorized obligation needed for correctness.
  Consolidate same-owner contiguous requirements only when no semantics are lost; do not mirror
  ledger rows, narrate citations, or repeat one obligation across fields.

Represent the complete source-defined horizon. Under-specified future work belongs in a
planned, deferred, or blocked phase without invented semantics. Permanent non-goals are exclusions; source-requested
research may be a planned outcome.

Keep contradictions explicit. Unless sources define precedence, never choose by order,
specificity, convention, or convenience. A gap exists only when externally observable behavior
for a source-mandated input cannot be expressed faithfully, even as an abstract relation or
precondition. An unspecified initializer, ownership derivation, encoding, algorithm, or internal
limit is implementation freedom unless the source makes it observable. Isolate the smallest
genuinely ambiguous obligation only when the remaining behavior stays complete, reachable, and
non-vacuous; otherwise keep the cohesive behavior non-ready. Cite both sides of contradictions and
block only their owner; do not spread status to adjacent work or dependents. Put a decision in
top-level `unresolved` only when no faithful decomposition or dependency graph can be produced
without resolving it. Otherwise keep the gap local to its phase.

## Source ownership and citations

Each distinct material requirement has one phase owner and one canonical source record. A global
invariant may cite its defining rule, but exactly one phase also cites it to establish it; others
apply its ID. `unresolved` is explanation, not another owner.

Promote a source-backed rule to a global invariant only when the source explicitly makes it an
unchanged cross-component rule and at least two production phase contracts consume it. Otherwise
keep it with its behavior owner. Its ID uses the phase-ID rule and its statement is the shortest
self-contained source clause. Oxide orders `oxide-verification-policy` first and the rest by ID.

Every source record must obey all of these rules:

- `path`: exact relative manifest path, never ROADMAP.md or the Oxide policy.
- `anchor`: complete displayed title of the containing heading, minus its Markdown marker and with
  numbering, case, punctuation, and wording unchanged. The normalized anchor title must occur
  exactly once in that file; for a repeated title use the nearest containing ancestor whose title
  occurs exactly once.
- `requirement`: literal contiguous source span supporting one atomic obligation. Preserve words,
  punctuation, links, code, modality, conditions, exceptions, and numbers. Never reconstruct prose
  from list or table fragments, paraphrase, splice, cross headings, or use ellipses. When authority
  is noncontiguous, emit separate records and keep the roadmap claim within their combined
  entailment. Structured-response escaping is encoding only: the decoded value preserves the
  selected source text.
- A quotation supports only what it entails; distinct source authority gets a separate record.

Before emitting each source record, isolate its heading scope, select the span, and verify canonical
containment in that scope after structured-response encoding. Repair failures; never emit a
best-effort paraphrase. When one cohesive contiguous span supports several obligations owned by the
same phase, cite its smallest enclosing span once rather than splitting or repeating it.

Citation fidelity is semantic, not typographic: ignore soft wrapping, cosmetic indentation widths,
heading-marker spelling, unordered-list glyph spelling, emphasis, comments, and table-rule styling.
Preserve heading boundaries and titles, list nesting and type, ordered ordinals and order, checkbox
state, and scoped blockquote, table, and code boundaries. Formatting cannot change the plan. For
identical canonical semantics emit only the lexicographically first `(path, anchor, requirement)`;
differences in authority, modality, condition, exception, precedence, or meaning are not duplicates.

## Readiness

Roadmap status and phase readiness differ. `status = "ready"` means the complete faithful plan is
approval-ready, even with planned/deferred phases; otherwise use `draft`. Phase readiness describes
only the contractibility of that phase's source-defined semantics. It never reports whether code,
proofs, checks, dependencies, or research have been completed. A dependency's readiness never
changes a dependent phase's readiness; dependency completion controls later execution eligibility.

Use `ready` when the phase's own behavior, material inputs/outputs/errors/bounds, and
source-entailed acceptance intent are precise enough to generate a contract. Use `planned` for a
named local semantic decision or elaboration, `deferred` when the source postpones the capability,
and `blocked` for a local contradiction or missing required product semantics. Isolate and name the
smallest missing obligation in that phase's scope or goals. Unknown implementation detail, proof
difficulty, empirical work, or an incomplete prerequisite alone does not prevent local readiness
or approval of an otherwise complete roadmap.

`ready_for_approval` is true exactly when `status = "ready"` and `unresolved` is empty.

## Verification policy

Policy profile {{ policy_profile_json }} with digest {{ policy_digest_json }} applies to every
phase. Use exactly one source-free universal invariant:

- id: `oxide-verification-policy`
- statement: `Production logic has meaningful contracts, component refinement, complete coverage,
  and exact-tree composition; trusted effects remain narrow and policy-free.`

Attach it to every phase as a fail-closed guard for coverage, trust, and exact-tree assurance; this
universal attachment does not turn empirical claims into formal theorems. Source-trace every
product invariant. Give logical production work meaningful proof/refinement goals, and give
empirical, research, or tooling phases source-backed experiment and qualification goals rather than
fake proofs. Never claim Verus proves performance.

